[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'm treating this as bounded. The filter only touches the existing activity page, so I'll give you a short design here
  in chat and skip the spec file. Say so if you think it needs more than that.

  The first decision is where the filter controls go on the page, and that's easier to show than describe. I'm sketching
  it in a browser tab, which should open now at:

  [94m]8;id=m5o9jm;http://localhost:54726/?key=7cc3ad0c7a906bcc7fc21c2deb7e1ba5beb3b88f4636e3140d89755208361998\http://localhost:54726/?key=7cc3ad0c7a906bcc7fc21c2deb7e1ba5beb3b88f4636e3140d89755208361998[39m]8;;\

  (The browser tab uses a lot of tokens, so tell me if you'd rather do this in the terminal.)

  The tab shows three placements. Each has the same table underneath:

  - [1mA: Inline filter bar.[0m A labelled "Event type" select and a "Failed sign-ins only" checkbox, with a live count like
    "Showing 48 of 48 events."
  - [1mB: Quick-filter row.[0m Four preset groups: All, Failed sign-ins, Sign-ins and sign-outs, and Account changes. Failed
    sign-ins take one click, but you can't pick a single type like "Password changed."
  - [1mC: Filter button with a popover.[0m This keeps the header clean, but finding failed sign-ins takes two clicks and the
    active filter is hidden until you open the panel.

  I recommend A. Failed sign-ins are what security cares about and what the support tickets were about, and A gets you
  there in one click while still letting you pick any of the nine event types.

  Have a look and tell me what you think here. You can also click an option in the browser to select it.

  One housekeeping item: the companion saves its files under [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in the repo, and there's no [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m entry
  for it yet. You may want to add one. I haven't touched [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m myself.

